I finally managed my first ascension, after years of on-and-off
nethacking:
4934992 deathkin-arc-hum-mal-law ascended to demigod-hood.

I don't have a dumplog, but I'll review the pertinent details as they
come to me:
1) I was playing this character at random, as the RNG saw fit, which
I usually never do (generally sticking to Wizards and dying quickly),
when I engrave-ID a wand of wishing on Dlvl 4.  This was really the
beginning of the end: GDSM, Grayswandir, b(BoH and "oR from Sokoban
and I was on my way, with some wishes to spare.
2) I found pretty decent loot throughout, only wishing for b[CoMR
with the Castle wand in my possession.  Cleared out Knox, bought gobs
of protection from a priest; then, converted his altar, killed him,
recovered my money, and went to the next priest.  Naked AC was -8
when I ran out of zorkmids and non-co-aligned priests.
3) Dropped into the Valley, saw a couple liches, got scared, killed
'em anyway.  Man I love Grayswandir + silver saber.  Recalling a monk
who lost a BoH to a lich, I returned to the castle and wished for
magic markers like a first-day kindergartener.  Had I seen any ?oGeno
yet?  Nope.  Did I still write one on the first try?  Oh yeah.  L, h,
and later ; fell to the power of the Genocide.  `The pen is mightier
than the sword', or something like that.
4) Gehennom was unremarkable; none of the demons summoned anyone, and
Grayswandir took care of them nicely, plus anyone else who got in the
way.  I dug tunnels between the stairs, looted and pillaged.  Also,
being an Archeologist, I kept every single valuable gem I found in
the game, and ascended with all of them.  Just because.
5) After tunneling like crazy, putting a nice stash by the stair on
Dlvl 1, alchemizing !oFH and writing ?oGD, enchanting all my armor to
+5 (giving me AC:-51), and reading more spoilers than is healthy,
I felt ready.  The wizard wouldn't fall to /oD for a bit, but
everybody has to die sometime.  Hacked my way through the zombies
in Moloch's sanctum, zapped the priests with /oTele, and I was the
proud new owner of the Amulet.
6) The escape went as hoped: Rodney showed up once and was irradiated
with pure energy once, the malignant aura left the important stuff
alone, and the mysterious force was only mildly bothersome, and then
only on the bottom few levels.  The climb beyond that was even more
quiet.
7) Having never seen the planes before, I was nervous; however, they
seemed laughable on this trip.  Earth and Air went quickly, reading
my confused scroll and running ahead of the monsters; I attempted the
same thing on Fire, but got bogged down amongst E and v, forcing me
to hack at them for a while until they got the idea.  Never did they
take me below 400HP (max was ~430).  Water found me slaying E left
and right, until finally when I read the scroll the portal was
practically right under me, and I levitated to it.
8) Famine on the left, Death in the middle, Pestilence on the right.
Might as well go right, Pestilence can suck a death ray... nope,
that's the chaotic altar.  Is my tame guardian angel dead yet?  Thank
Quetzalcoatl, now it's ring of conflict time.  Much easier to move
about.  Whack Death... Middle altar?  Nope, that's Camaxtli.  Guess
third time is the charm... Famine falls to my quick-draw skills,
a couple whacks, a couple zaps and there it is.
9) If Nethack has any weakness, I would say it is the boredom of
wading through messages when there are too many monsters at once.
Fire, Air, and Astral took gobs of patience, watching all the
monsters miss me in my ridiculous armour.  Anyone have a solution to
this?  Reduce the message output when creatures attack each other,
perhaps?  Still doesn't solve the problem of too much spacebar usage
when you are surrounded.
10) Despite the various nasties on Astral, my HP never dropped below
200, and had naturally regenerated to ~380 when I ascended.  I didn't
go toe-to-toe with many enemies, preferring to run around them, but I
would Grayswandir them in a second if they were in the way.  Are they
really that easy?  Was it my armor?  All the alchemizing for !oFH
seemed a big waste of time.

So there you have it; the no-holds-barred scramble to the top, and
all thanks to a /oW.



